      explanation: 'Each testis contains three cell populations with three different jobs: Leydig cells, which produce sex hormones; spermatogenic cells, which make sperm; and Sertoli cells, which give mechanical and nutritional support. Spermatogenesis begins at the outermost cell layer of a seminiferous tubule and proceeds toward the lumen. Spermatogonia, the stem cells, divide by mitosis to produce two daughter cells; one remains a spermatogonium to keep the stem line, and the other differentiates into a primary spermatocyte. Primary spermatocytes begin meiosis and form secondary spermatocytes with 46 chromosomes; the secondary spermatocytes differentiate into spermatids, the immature gametes carrying 23. Spermatids then differentiate into spermatozoa, which lose contact with the wall of the seminiferous tubule and enter the fluid in the lumen. During meiosis the maternal and paternal chromosomes come together and some exchange of genetic material occurs between the chromatids of a chromosome pair — crossing over, which increases genetic variation among offspring. The finished spermatozoon is stripped down for transport. It has a head of about 5 micrometres carrying the nucleus and the enzyme-packed acrosome, a middle piece of about 5 micrometres wrapped in a mitochondrial spiral to power the tail, and a fibrous tail around 55 micrometres long. A mature sperm lacks ER, Golgi, lysosomes, centrioles and other intracellular structures; the loss of these organelles reduces sperm size and mass, and the sperm must instead absorb nutrients — fructose — from the surrounding fluid. Sertoli cells, also called nurse cells, secrete the tubule fluid: rich in proteins, enzymes, nutrients, androgens such as testosterone, ions and bicarbonates, the fluid enters the lumen to stimulate and nourish the developing sperm, helps flush sperm into the epididymis, and its Sertoli cells engulf any damaged germ cells. Sertoli cells also synthesize two proteins both needed for spermatogenesis: androgen-binding protein (ABP), which binds testosterone and increases its concentration around the developing sperm, and inhibin, which stimulates the pituitary gland to inhibit FSH secretion. Hormonal control runs on two routes. Testosterone is required to stimulate meiosis and early spermatid maturation, and it is secreted by the Leydig cells after stimulation by LH — LH to Leydig cells to testosterone. FSH enhances spermatogenesis through the action of the Sertoli cells, which are stimulated to make ABP and so concentrate testosterone where it is needed; FSH ensures optimal fertility. Testosterone then acts far beyond the tubule: it maintains spermatogenesis, enlarges the penis and testes while scrotal skin becomes wrinkled and pigmented, drives facial, pubic and body hair growth, enlarges the vocal cords and larynx to lower the voice pitch, increases body growth, increases muscle mass through raised protein synthesis, grows the long bones while increasing calcium retention, and stimulates red blood cell formation (erythropoiesis) — the reason adult male haemoglobin runs higher than female.',
