      explanation: 'Sperm leave the testis by a fixed route: from the seminiferous tubules they move to the rete testis, then through the efferent ductules to the epididymis. The epididymis is the site of sperm maturation and storage, and it is not a passive pipe: it is lined with smooth muscle that contracts in rhythmic peristalsis, the sperm arrive immature and nonmotile at first, and its lining secretes a fluid containing nutrients, enzymes and hormones. Sperm pass through it for about 2 weeks; if there is no ejaculation, they are stored in the caudal portion, the tail end. Once sperm leave the epididymis, 90% of the fluid is reabsorbed there, resulting in a highly concentrated sperm suspension, and the sperm then enter the vas deferens (ductus deferens). In ejaculation, spermatozoa move from the epididymis through the ductus deferens, the ejaculatory duct and the urethra — but at that point sperm are still only a small fraction of what leaves, because the accessory sex organs contribute almost all the volume. The seminal vesicles contribute about 60% of the volume of semen: their secretion contains fructose, the energy for sperm, prostaglandins that stimulate smooth-muscle contractions in both male and female tracts, and it is slightly alkaline, helping to neutralize the acids in the vagina and in prostatic secretions. The prostate gland contributes 20–30% of the volume of semen, is slightly acidic, and contains seminalplasmin, a protein with antibiotic properties that may help prevent urinary tract infections in males. The Cowper glands (bulbourethral glands) secrete a thick, alkaline mucus that helps neutralize any urinary acids remaining in the urethra and lubricates the glans, or tip, of the penis. The release of the whole mixture is under autonomic control with three named events. Erection is due to an increase in parasympathetic outflow over the pelvic nerves, which fills the erectile tissue with blood. Emission is the movement of semen into the urethra, due to an increase in sympathetic nerve activity. Ejaculation is the forceful expulsion of semen from the urethra, also under sympathetic nervous system control: contraction of smooth muscles in the tubules, the seminal vesicle and prostate, and the muscles at the base of the penis all take part. So the autonomic split is memorable — parasympathetic for point, sympathetic for shot — and erection is the only one of the three events on the parasympathetic side.',
